## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: every rate integrated
against the delta time the game is given, so an interval of game time reaches the
same state however it was divided into frames. Two more properties make it
driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
  time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
  renderer. Whether a segment may be added is decided from the board and the beams
  alone, never from anything the renderer holds.
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses runs off a generator seeded from
  `state.rngState`, and it keeps its whole generator state in that field, so
  reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly. A
  build that uses no randomness simply never reads it.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the
game, and the surface carries no operation for the registered actions or for the
overlay. That layer delivers the actions the game registers from the key bindings
this specification fixes, it reports the pointer position in the stage's logical
units along with the press and release edges, and it draws and toggles the debug
overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them.
The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it
drives the actions the game registers, it reports the pointer position in the
stage's logical units along with the press and release edges, and it draws and
toggles the debug overlay.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them.
The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it
drives the actions the game registers, it hands the game the pointer's ordered
samples in the stage's logical units with the press and release edges, and it
draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Refract's, under Diagnostics below.

The surface's pointer operations do not stand in for that pointer. They feed the
same input path it feeds, so a posed press and a player's press are the same event
to the game, and nothing is bypassed: the hit radius, the grab rules, and the
limits all run exactly as they do for a player.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`REFRACT_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans. `x` and `y`
are in the stage's logical units, and `col` and `row` are cell coordinates as
`specs/board.md` defines them.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<RefractState>`. A pose returns the next `RefractState`, built from
the one it was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read.
The headings below name the parameters that follow the state.
{{else}}

Each operation takes only the parameters its heading names and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it
read and changes nothing.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes only
when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is
how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent frame left.

### `advance(seconds, frames)`

Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth
`seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each is a
real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the game's own
completion test and animation produce the result and the canvas reflects it.

Every rate in this game is per second and integrated against the frame's delta
time, so `advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same second of game time
and must reach the same outcome, beyond the drift a change in step size explains.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a scenario that must be reproducible calls
`setAutoStep(false)` first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `reset(options)`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `reset(state, options)`
{{else}}
### `reset(options)`
{{/if}}

Restores every declared field of the game's state to its title-screen value: the
title screen with its first menu item highlighted, `mode` back to `"campaign"`, no
board in play, every beam empty, no trace live, the pointer reported as up,
`simTime` at `0`, campaign progress cleared, so no board is recorded solved and
only the opening board is unlocked, and the cascade progression back to its
opening state, `solvedCount` at `0` and `tier` at `1`. `options.seed` seeds
`rngState`, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`).

`muted` is deliberately untouched: muting is a player preference the runtime owns.

Because it restores exactly the declared fields, any state of your own that a
frame depends on is derivable from them.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape below.
It changes nothing.

### `startMode(mode)`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `startMode(state, mode)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape below.
It changes nothing.

### `startMode(mode)`
{{/if}}

Poses the choice of a mode from the title menu, with `mode` being `"campaign"` or
`"cascade"`. `state.mode` becomes that mode and the game moves to the screen that
mode opens on, exactly as choosing its menu item does, as
`specs/modes/campaign.md` and `specs/modes/cascade.md` describe. It leaves the
progression of both modes as it stands and `simTime` as it is, so a run from a
clean progression is a `reset` followed by this.

The game runs from there: the board the mode opens with is chosen, and generated
where the mode generates it, through the game's own code.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `loadBoard(board)`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `loadBoard(state, board)`
{{else}}
### `loadBoard(board)`
{{/if}}

Poses an arbitrary board and moves to `playing` with every beam empty and no trace
live. `board` is the board notation `specs/board.md` defines, one string per row.
Boards up to `GRID_MAX_COLS` (`7`) by `GRID_MAX_ROWS` (`6`) are accepted, and cell
centers follow the formula in `specs/board.md`, so a posed board sits on the stage
exactly where a played board of the same dimensions would.

This is the surface's main lever: it puts the game on a chosen board without
playing to it. A board posed this way is a board like any other, and the rules in
`specs/beams.md` apply to it unchanged.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `pointerDown(x, y)`, `pointerMove(x, y)`, `pointerUp()`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `pointerDown(state, x, y)`, `pointerMove(state, x, y)`, `pointerUp(state)`
{{else}}
### `pointerDown(x, y)`, `pointerMove(x, y)`, `pointerUp()`
{{/if}}

Report a press, a move, and a release at a logical stage position. A press targets
the node whose cell center is within `NODE_HIT_R` (`44`) of `(x, y)` and begins a
trace by exactly the grab rules in `specs/controls.md`. A move extends the beam to
an adjacent node, or retracts it, under those same rules, and a move the limits
refuse changes nothing and leaves the trace live. The release ends the trace and
leaves the beam as drawn.

**Each of the three takes effect immediately, when it is called, rather than being
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
sampled once per update.** The press, the move, or the release is resolved in the
state the call returns rather than deferred to the next update.
No frame need pass between them, so a whole route can be drawn from code without
advancing the game at all.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
sampled once per update.** The press, the move, or the release is resolved in the
state the call returns rather than deferred to the next update.
Each returns the state that press, move, or release left behind, and the next call
is handed that state, so a whole route can be drawn from code without a frame
passing between the calls.
{{else}}
sampled once per frame.** The press, the move, or the release is resolved against
the live state before the call returns rather than deferred to the next frame,
through the same per-sample resolution the player controller feeds each frame.
No frame need pass between them, so a whole route can be drawn from code without
advancing the game at all.
{{/if}}

A move that satisfies R9 solves the board and ends the trace on the spot, as
`specs/beams.md` states. The release that follows begins nothing and changes
nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `trace(cells)`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `trace(state, cells)`
{{else}}
### `trace(cells)`
{{/if}}

Sugar over the three pointer operations: a press at the first cell's center, a
move to each remaining cell's center in turn, then a release. `cells` is a list of
`{ col, row }`, and each center comes from the formula in `specs/board.md`.
Because each of those operations takes effect as it is made, one call draws the
whole route.

Running entirely through the pointer path, a `trace` is subject to every limit a
hand-drawn trace is. A list the limits refuse part way through leaves the beam
ending at the last segment they permitted.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `clear()`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clear(state)`
{{else}}
### `clear()`
{{/if}}

The same as the `clear` action in `specs/controls.md`: every beam on the board is
emptied and any live trace ends. It applies on the `playing` screen alone.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A typical scenario calls `loadBoard` to pose the board wanted, `trace` once per
channel to draw its beam, and reads the result from `snapshot()`.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
A typical scenario applies `loadBoard` through
`engine.apply((s) => debug.loadBoard(s, board))`, then a `trace` per channel the
same way to draw its beam, and reads the result from `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
A typical scenario calls `engine.debug.loadBoard(board)` to pose the board
wanted, `engine.debug.trace(cells)` once per channel to draw its beam, and reads
the result from `engine.debug.snapshot()`.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "select" | "playing" | "solved" | "complete",
  mode: "campaign" | "cascade",
  boardIndex: <number>,        // the campaign board being played, counted from 0
  solvedBoards: [<number>],    // the campaign indices solved so far, ascending
  unlockedCount: <number>,     // how many campaign boards are open to the player
  solvedCount: <number>,       // boards solved in the current cascade run
  tier: <number>,              // the tier the next cascade board is generated at
  board: {
    cols: <number>,
    rows: <number>,
    nodes: [
      { col, row, x, y,
        kind: "emitter" | "lens" | "crystal",
        channel: "triangle" | "square" | "diamond" | null,
        charges: <number | null>,
        spent: <number | null> },
    ],
  },
  beams: {
    // one entry per channel present
    triangle: { cells: [{ col, row }], complete: <boolean> },
  },
  solved: <boolean>,
  tracing: { channel: <string>, live: { col, row } } | null,
  pointer: { x: <number>, y: <number>, down: <boolean> },
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,   // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

The shape is fixed, and every field is present whatever the mode is. `select` and
`complete` are screens Campaign reaches and Cascade does not, as
`specs/modes/campaign.md` and `specs/modes/cascade.md` state, and the four
progress fields belong one pair to each mode. A field the current mode does not
use reports its resting value rather than going missing:

| Field | Resting value |
| --- | --- |
| `boardIndex` | `0` |
| `solvedBoards` | empty |
| `unlockedCount` | `1` |
| `solvedCount` | `0` |
| `tier` | `1` |

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot reports is
what the game holds. Four are derived rather than stored:

| Field | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| a node's `x`, `y` | the cell center formula in `specs/board.md` |
| a crystal's `spent` | the crossings the drawn beams have begun on it |
| a beam's `complete` | R6 and R7 in `specs/beams.md` |
| `solved` | R9 in `specs/beams.md` |

A node's `channel` is `null` for a crystal, and its `charges` and `spent` are
`null` for an emitter and for a lens. `beams` carries one entry per channel
present on the board and no entry for a channel the board does not use.

A beam's `cells` lists the cells its segments join, **in the order they were
drawn**, so the last cell is the end the most recent trace worked from and the one
a resumed trace extends. `tracing` is `null` whenever no trace is live, and
`tracing.live` names the cell of the live end, which is that last cell.

Three fields need the game to keep them honest every frame:

- `pointer` mirrors the position the pointer input reports and whether the
  pointer is currently pressed, refreshed in every update.
- `muted` mirrors the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every update, since the
  runtime owns muting and this is the game's readable copy of it.
- `simTime` accumulates the delta time of every update, whatever the screen.

## Diagnostics

The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources. Register at least the current `screen` and `mode`, the board's
`cols` and `rows`, each channel's beam length and whether it is complete, each
crystal's spent and total charges, whether the board is solved, and the pointer
position, the same facts the snapshot reports. Keep each one short enough to read
on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the
game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), and it reads the game without changing it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Refract's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<RefractState>`, and reads off that argument: a source that closed
over the state `initialize` built would report the title screen forever, because
every frame leaves a new value behind. Drawing the panel, toggling it, and keeping
it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Refract's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of
no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the
world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the
panel and toggling it are the engine's.
{{/if}}
